//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.option_flatten — `std.option`'s sixth helper, parked outside the
//! facade for one grammar fact. Stability tier: x (nursery, D31 clock: it
//! graduates INTO `std.option` verbatim, or is deleted, at the stdc02
//! closeout).
//!
//! Why it is here and not in `std.option`, in one sentence: `flatten` is
//! the only helper whose type nests a row inside a row
//! (`T ! {none} ! {none}`), wolfc's grammar admits exactly one `!` per
//! type in every position and calls the nested form `fail(E0201)` at
//! parse, and one rejected body poisons a module for every importer —
//! so keeping it beside the other five would cost them their clean static
//! ladder (F-0039). lupin parses and executes the nested row, which is
//! how the body below was written and tested.
//!
//! Nothing else is different: this is the reviewed signature from
//! `std.option`'s header, with the same contract, the same tag, and the
//! same one-executing-lane story. It moves back on the pin that makes
//! wolfc's `type` production right-recursive, in that pin's own commit.

/// One layer of absence removed: a doubly-absent value is absent, and an
/// inner miss is absent too. The two misses are indistinguishable by
/// construction — `none` carries no payload (§2), so there is nothing to
/// tell an outer miss from an inner one, and a caller who needs to know
/// wants two named tags rather than nesting.
///
/// Rows: raises `none` when either layer is absent.
/// Total otherwise: no traps.
///
/// Example (prose — a row value has no literal, F-0038; runnable in
/// `tests/x/option_flatten/flatten_propagate.lu`): with
/// `fn nest(x: int) -> int ! {none} ! {none}`,
/// `option_flatten.flatten(nest(16)) else 0 - 1` is `4` and
/// `option_flatten.flatten(nest(6)) else 0 - 1` is `-1`.
pub fn flatten[T](v: T ! {none} ! {none}) -> T ! {none} {
    let inner = v else |_| { return none }
    inner
}
